Controller resource/image serialization checks
==============================================

Run from a checkout without building or booting the kernel:

  sh tools/testing/selftests/multikernel/image_lock_test.sh
  CC=gcc CFLAGS=-O2 sh tools/testing/selftests/multikernel/image_lock_test.sh
  CC=clang CFLAGS='-O1 -g -fsanitize=address,undefined -fno-sanitize-recover=all -fno-omit-frame-pointer' \
    sh tools/testing/selftests/multikernel/image_lock_test.sh

The runner extracts verbatim production file-load, allocation/error cleanup,
MK image release, lookup, exec, instance lookup/state and overlay-rmdir functions.
It uses real pthread mutexes and a second thread entering the actual overlay
rmdir control flow during load, exec, unload or failed-load cleanup. The
contender announces its lock attempt and cannot mutate until the image operation
unlocks. Without the patch, the initial harness reported 166 failures in 842
checks, including actual contender progress inside an image operation.

Assertions check kexec -> resource -> registry acquisition, reverse release,
no image work under the registry lock, cleanup before unlocking and exclusion
from overlay rollback. Load/exec failure stages, unknown IDs, empty CPU sets,
mapping/allocation failure and early permission/flag/busy exits are covered.
Normal/crash images retain their existing absence of a resource lock; cleanup
is now inside kexec_lock, as it already is for legacy kexec_load.

Command-line cleanup uses the extracted kimage_file_free_cmdline helper and a
small tracked allocation supplied by the segment-preparation boundary. Early
preparation, validation and control/swap-page failures must free it for normal,
crash and multikernel images (crash images have no swap page). Successful loads
must retain it until unload. Post-load cleanup intentionally retains this buffer;
the early allocation unwind must release it before freeing the image directly.
This is a host cleanup regression check, not a kernel syscall reproduction.

Kernel allocation, segment copying, firmware, pool mutation, instance registry
storage and kernfs removal are controlled host boundaries. The real MK release
and can-free helpers are exercised; the general kimage_free dispatcher is a
boundary. The
fixture contains only the fields reached by the extracted functions. Kernel
sign-compare and unused-argument diagnostics from those functions are excluded
from the host compiler's -Werror policy, not from kernel build validation.
The host mutexes check control-flow locking, not kernel lockdep or scheduling.

Remaining release gates
----------------------

This mutex covers local controller overlay/baseline resource changes and MK
image load, exec and cleanup. Incoming resource workers now refuse a contended
resource lock with -EBUSY rather than mutate through a controller operation.
Active Image unload and execution are refused. An execution request cannot
bypass halt settlement merely because individual CPUs look parked; it returns
-EBUSY before confirmation, Image writes, manifest updates or ring resets.
The ACTIVE rejection is covered even when the substituted confirmation would
succeed. An uncertain failed start must first confirm
all assigned/tracked CPUs stopped. Manifest-finalization failure prevents
starting the Image, and a partial start failure retains FAILED state rather
than publishing a reusable LOADED state. These paths are covered here with
controlled stop/start boundaries, not actual firmware execution.

Halt/HALTED/force-halt settlement and destruction are tested separately by
halt_settlement_test.sh, teardown_test.sh and memory_teardown_test.sh. They do
not cancel queued remote work or qualify cross-kernel producer retirement.

A substituted successful firmware start does not prove CPU execution or safe
reclamation after a partial start. Local threading, sanitizers and kernel links
do not replace x86 or QEMU/OpenSBI runtime, failure-injection and re-spawn tests.
